What does this program print? (Assume the standard headers from the lesson, fork succeeds, and wait is not interrupted.)

```c
int x = 1;
int main(void) {
  if (fork() == 0) {
    x = 2;
    printf("c %p %d\n", (void*)&x, x);
    return 0;
  }
  wait(NULL);
  printf("p %p %d\n", (void*)&x, x);
}
```

Both loops run over a 1 GiB array of 4-byte ints with 4 KiB pages, on a CPU whose TLBs cover only a few MiB.

```c
/* A */
for (i = 0; i < N; i++)
  s += a[i];
/* B */
for (i = 0; i < N; i += 1024)
  s += a[i];
```

Assume page alignment, cold TLB state for each loop, scalar loads, no translation prefetching and no interfering evictions. How do they compare in this model?

A program does this, then exits normally:

```c
int fd = open("cfg.bin", O_RDWR);
char *p = mmap(NULL, 4096,
  PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
  MAP_PRIVATE, fd, 0);
p[0] = 'X';
```

What is the first byte of `cfg.bin` on disk afterwards? Assume standard headers, successful open/mmap, a nonempty file, and no other writer.
